  "teamId": 116,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Detroit Tigers arrived with modest expectations, and for much of the first half they did little to change that perception. A solid March gave way to a .500 April and a losing May and June, as the rotation struggled with injury and inconsistency and the bullpen — particularly Jason Foley and Shelby Miller — too often surrendered crucial leads. Daulton Varsho's three-run blast off Foley in late May and a brutal Mitch Haniger double in August bookended a stretch of heartbreaking late-game losses that kept the Tigers from ever fully trusting themselves.\n\nThe turnaround began quietly in July and then roared to life in August and September. Young players like Colt Keith, Wenceel Pérez, Justyn-Henry Malloy, and Parker Meadows gave the lineup a fresh, electric identity, and the pitching — anchored by a transcendent Tarik Skubal — steadied into one of the American League's stingiest staffs. A 17-11 August and a scorching 17-8 September turned what looked like a lost season into a genuine postseason push, carried by moments like Parker Meadows' walk-off grand slam against San Diego in early September and a series of clutch multi-run hits from an increasingly confident young core.\n\nThe Wild Card round brought the Tigers to Houston, where they knocked off the Astros in two games to advance to the ALDS for the first time in years. Andy Ibáñez delivered a go-ahead two-run double in the clinching game, and Parker Meadows homered to set the tone. Detroit had earned its October moment through sheer late-season will.\n\nThe ALDS against Cleveland proved a bridge too far. Tarik Skubal was brilliant in Game 1, inducing a key double play to protect a lead, and Kerry Carpenter's three-run homer gave Detroit an early lift. But the Guardians, deep and relentless, chipped away. David Fry homered twice in the series, and Lane Thomas delivered a crushing grand slam off a tiring Tiger bullpen in the deciding game. The Tigers fell, their remarkable late surge ending not with disgrace but with the sting of knowing how close they had come.",
  "chapters": [
